As should be obvious for anything posted in this medium, the following 
represents just my opinion. I do not claim to be an authority on the topics 
discussed.

<Invective about NDP Nazis from Keith Medcalf deleted>

While I agree that the NDP as a group should be tried for (high?) treason, 
there a few things that should be pointed out, here:

- Policticians have nothing to do with the imposition of the ban. That was 
done by the courts.

- One of the Solicitor-General's role is to enforce the wishes of the courts, 
theoritically, regardless of how she feels about them.

- The ban only lasts until the end of the Paul Bernardo/Teale's trial. If the 
jury was going to be sequestered (which I don't think happens very often in 
Canada), I would guess that there's no reason why the judge won't lift the ban 
as soon as the jury are safely out of the media's sway.

I was once the victim of a grossly inaccurate newspaper article (many things 
were attributed to me in an interview that I never said), so I can appreciate 
that the media can be a source of great misinformation and prejudice. 
Remember, the judge's motivations are that he honestly believes that Teale 
will not get a fair trial if the ban is not in place.

We've had laws for years which prevent the disclosure of the identifies of 
criminals, either because of their age or for fear of identifying their 
victims. Nobody has complained about so loudly about those. In the U.S., if a 
case is settled out of court, the records of trial (up to the point of 
settlement) are often sealed, and are unavailable for anyone. How many 
thousands of people have been hurt by not having access to evidence of wrong 
doing by companies (safety violations, etc.)?

The concept of absolute rights does not exist in Canada. In the U.S., many 
people interpret the 2nd Amendment to mean that they have the absolute, 
inalienable right to own and possess whatever weapon they choose. That's fine 
for them, but in Canada, society chose instead to impose limits on what 
private citizens can own. There is no indication that the fabric of our 
society is unravelling in the face of our social choices.

No society has complete freedom for its citizens. All information we receive 
is filtered to varying degrees, slanting it in favour of opinions of the 
carrier. Reporters have pre-conceived notions of a whether a defendant is 
guilty or innocent (not, you will note, 'guilty' or 'not guilty' as the 
courts try to decide), and this can colour their judgement in what they choose 
to report, and the 'spin' they put on it.

Information in our society is too bountious and complex for us to try and 
digest it all. Instead, we pay others to do it for us, and to provide us with 
a brief summary.

Having ranted all that, I must say I wish the ban had never been put in place. 
I agree with the sentiment that everything must be done to ensure that Teale 
gets a fair trial. I just don't believe the ban is going to do it.

Whew! I feel better, now.

------------------------------------------------------------
Huw Leonard
