(did that get your attention?)

It is reasonably common practice by system administrators to drop the
distribution of groups like alt.sex and alt.drugs due to the controversial
nature of those groups, probably based quite substantially upon the
prejudices of the system administrator responsible.  The content of those
groups is typically dismissed out of hand as being entirely useless.

This is not so.

Recently there was the successful completion of an academic study using
material gained from recruiting volunteers from alt.drugs and related
newsgroups.  The study was done by a researcher at NIMH and was concerned
with the interactions between psychedelics and antidepressants, and the
implications for the mechanism of action of both psychedelics and
antidepressants in the brain.  The results of this study are probably
going to be written up in the journals _Behavior and Brain Research_ and
either the _Journal of Clinical Psychopharmacology_ or
_Neuropsychopharmacology_ (pending acceptance).

Had alt.drugs been dropped from the news feed the researcher would not
have had access to the number of volunteers that she did.  Her ability
to perform her *job* would therefore have been restricted by a
prejudiced system administrator.

System administrators who fail to distribute groups like alt.sex and
alt.drugs should carefully consider exactly who they are providing a
newsfeed to, and if they could be failing to carry out their job by
doing so.  It is not, unfortunately for them, simply hormonally charged
and disturbed freshman who get on in september and immediately gravitate
to these newsgroups.  You may wind up with some prof or researchers who
are academically interested in those groups.

I sincerely hope that if you censor the groups that those academians
wind up harassing you verbally on the phone, or contacting your boss...

You deserve it.

Have a nice day...

=)

