Mitch, Joshua...

Gentlemen....

Could it possibly be (as Joshua has pointed out) that the reason alt 
groups are not being carried is due to system limitations, as opposed to 
concerns about content?

If controversial newsgroups are being dropped >because< there are content 
concerns (I can imagine the Robertson/Wildmon looney fringe writing 
letters to the local college campus DEMANDING that such groups be 
dropped, with the usual whiny exuses ("we don't want our tax money 
supporting filth, etc.")) then we've got an issue.  But could it also be 
that due to bandwidth/system/storage limitations, wildly populuar 
newsgroups like alt.sex.* get dropped because they stress system resources?

Sometimes some digging is necessary to find out what the real reason for 
the action is.  I know that in Washington State a few years back there 
were concerns being raised by the pc left and the whacko right (strange 
bedfellows, but both with a pathological fear of human sexuality) about 
the UW carrying alt.sex newsgroups and "racy" binaries.  This is cause 
for concern by those opposed to censorship.  OTOH, sometimes a sysadmin 
has to make choices based on limited resources on what to carry.  When 
that is the case, alt newsgroups >seem< to bee the place to cut (along 
with other obvious choices like rec) as they're not central to the 
mission of the university and the internet >as originally conceived<.

Mitch seems to be arguing that the mission has changed (and I agree with 
him) but Joshua points out that sometimes resource limitations impose 
limits on what can be physically accomplished.

Motivations are sometimes difficult to discover, but sometimes a cigar is 
just a cigar.


